Erestorm was started in April 2010. I chose a drow in the hope to later 
use his special ability to put Demogorgon to sleep. Never happened, of 
course. But using the wand of fire and finding one of teleportation 
soon, he survived long enough for nice things to happen. There was an 
early armor shop with jump boots and an altar on Dlvl 2, which 
presumably gave him the athame Firewall before he expired his wand of 
fire (my notes are not detailed enough to mention Firewall, but it is 
the first gift for flame mages). Afterwards, one-turn-Elbereth was 
available in tight situations. As usual, there was the problem of magic 
protection, which he could not get in the early game. Of course, he 
stumbled into a polymorph trap (twice), but in one case, he became a 
ruby golem, which meant no major problems due to the polymorph (except 
for a lost studded leather armor and elven cloak). Desperate for good 
armor, he finally took to dipping a wand of digging into fountains on 
early dungeon levels. He summoned a peaceful water demon (after some 
nastier events) giving him a gray dragon scale mail. Later, the same 
trick led to his being equipped with the Bat from Hell. So a quite 
ascendable character, albeit a bit low on hit points, never exceeding 
120. He did Grund's stronghold and got some firearms there and did the 
neutral quest. He had even eaten a black dragon from the Wyrm Caves to 
obtain disintegration resistance. But dragons were still very dangerous 
in melee, despite the Bat (they are much stronger in slashem than in 
nethack). However, he found it was time now to finish the Wyrm Caves. He 
used some bullets from a distance and finished off the dragons with the 
Bat. But obviously, each adult dragon was a major challenge, and this 
was a suspenseful time.

Now, in earlier games I had learned to respect rot worms and my initial 
approach was to always kill them from a distance. They can make you 
sick, and they are easy to kill even with a dagger. Lately, I had become 
somewhat less careful, because they always die in one hit. All my 
attention was focused on the dragons, and I killed the rot worms as I 
passed (no problem with the Bat). Having lost quite some hit points to 
dragons, I was very careful to try and kill the last one with my 
submachine gun, before he could get at me. Finally, he was dead, too, 
and triumphantly I wandered around the battle field to look for loot. 
Then came the message "You die from your illness. DYWYPI?" What? I had 
not even noted to be ill!  And that was of course the problem. One of 
the rot worms, presumably the last one that I killed, must have bitten 
my character, and I did not notice, focusing on hit points rather than 
on the right side of the screen. I must have overlooked the first and 
only warning during the battle -- there was a message overload... My 
character must have lived for twenty or more turns after the bite, so 
there was plenty of time to cure himself, in principle, as he had a 
fully enchanted unicorn horn...  I was so frustrated that I decided not 
to make another attempt that year. Moreover, I was too busy with other 
things to play in 2011 and most of 2012. Only towards the end of the 
year, in November, I decided to try another flame mage, maybe to ascend 
him between Christmas or New Year (when there is time for playing :-) ).

But I had not played for a long time, so my game skills were a bit 
rusty. Somehow, I had decided that all my attempts would now have names 
starting with "Fire...". Firefly was the first. His problem was that he 
did not find sufficiently many good items in the initial phase of the 
game. His wand of fire saved his butt a few times, but finally was empty 
without any good weapon in sight. As I said, my skills were rusty, so I 
mostly used the wand for direct killing, instead of burning Elbereth 
into the ground, which lasts long and may save you several times. 
Moreover, I let my hell hound pet kill the priest in mine town. The 
altar was coaligned, so there was absolutely no reason to let this 
happen -- but I had not expected a dog-like creature to attack a priest. 
Anyway, the end came soon. A thief stole my wand of polymorph, carried 
openly, for lack of a sack, zapped himself and became a vampire mage. 
Apparently, he was too surprised to attack me immediately, so I could 
escape downstairs to look for my pet that was one level deeper. Then I 
had to decide: continue and most likely die below level 8 (having 
neither a good AC nor a good weapon) or go upstairs and run for the 
(close by) stairs towards the main dungeon -- hoping to reach Sokoban. 
Obviously, going back up was the wrong decision. The vampire mage, 
invisible in the meantime, drained my character; I had time for one last 
prayer (the invisible creature pulled back indeed), but no more help was 
given, so I died...

The next was Fireball. There was an early altar, so he managed to get 
Firewall and seemed to turn promising. He survived for quite some time 
engraving Elbereth liberally, but had no means for generating many 
sacrifices to obtain a better weapon than an athame (with an additional 
small bonus against non-fire-resistant monsters). But he had telepathy 
and a blindfold, so he could check at the beginning of each level, if 
there were particular dangers. On Dlvl 7, there was a zoo that he wanted 
to try cleaning out. In the zoo, there was a floating eye. After 
entering the zoo and noting that his pets were waking up the monsters 
too fast, he wanted to retreat. Of course, he knew that he should not 
approach the floating eye without his blindfold on. It was simply a slip 
of finger. I hit the wrong key and moved into a direction that I had not 
intended. The floating eye froze Fireball, and he did not manage to move 
again to engrave Elbereth, when a baby dragon started pounding on him...

This was certainly a stupid death, although the stupidity did not reside 
in my brain but rather in my fingers. So Firepin was started. In a way, 
he was much luckier than all his predecessors. There was an altar on 
Dlvl 2. There was a magic whistle in mine town. And there was a mall on 
Dlvl 5, with a big general store, useful for identifying items once you 
could pay for it. He got Firewall from the altar before his wand of fire 
was empty. Sokoban gave him a bag of holding. He found a wand of create 
monster and continued to sacrifice. The second gift was Deluder. Doh.  A 
cloak of displacement, giving you also stealth. Somewhat useful in early 
game, but I would have preferred a good artifact weapon. Got some 
elementals as pets. But they were not very strong in fights and died 
soon (made a mental note that a lawful character would have been better, 
because his god sends him nicer minions). And then a sacrifice yielded: 
Mjollnir. Now some means of magic protection and he would be a definite 
ascension candidate.  So what to do to to avoid the polymorph traps 
until then? This was the greatest worry. A polymorph trap could 
completely ruin this game. The solution was to tame as many pets as 
possible and to keep them next to you with the whistle. Then make sure 
not to step on a square on which none of your pets has been before. This 
worked nicely. Firepin proceeded slowly, having between three and seven 
pets all the time. Then found a wand that he tried to engrave-identify: 
"You may wish for an object!" O.k., that solved the problem of armor. 
After wishing for the obligatory two blessed scrolls of charging, our 
hero had a gray dragon scale mail. Now, he was surely ascendable...

Again, the demise came in the Wyrm Caves. And even earlier than for 
Erestorm. First, I killed a black dragon. Then tried to eat its corpse 
for disintegration resistance. Was interrupted while being satiated. 
Killed the intruder (a baby dragon). Now, years ago, when starting to 
play nethack, I *always* avoided continuing to eat when satiated. But in 
the meantime I had read (or believed to have read...) in a spoiler that 
when you are interrupted eating a corpse, you can finish that particular 
corpse without choking. (Similar to the situation when you lose 
consciousness eating bad food but can finish the partially eaten food 
ration afterwards without falling unconscious again.) I was so sure that 
I did not interrupt the game to verify the information. Well, that 
information was wrong. Firepin choked immediately. My second slashem 
character to find a wand of wishing before the castle. Neither of them 
ascended... So I should probably consider it a bad omen...

I was shocked, but not half as badly as in the case of Erestorm. This 
was definitely a very stupid death, but it was based on a 
misinformation. Well, it was a pity to lose such a promising character. 
Probably, I would have to make four or five more tries.

Initially, this seemed to be borne out by Firenail, because his progress 
was very slow. But there was an altar on Dlvl 1. Unfortunately, he 
needed about six attempts to convert it. There was no mall and there 
were only two smallish shops (not counting delicatessen) in mine town. 
No magic lamp, not even a magic candle. After getting to the bottom of 
the mines, unfortunately not the wine cellar, which would at least have 
given some holy water, he had a few valuable gems. The hard white one 
was identified to be diamond and sold in the general shop both for money 
to buy some AC and for identification of items, in particular all 
valuable gems. It was tedious to have my pet steal back the gems without 
a magic whistle, so I left some of them there.  Good things were an 
armor shop on Dlvl 6, with the shop keeper offering armor works, and a 
big general shop on Dlvl 12. Sokoban gave a bag of holding and the 
oracle level held a polymorph trap, revealed by one of my pets. I used 
the trick again to try and only step on squares occupied by one of my 
pets before, to avoid polymorph traps. But it was very tedious without a 
magic whistle, and I spent some 50000 turns above Dlvl 20. Things became 
a bit easier after I found a ring of polymorph control, because now I 
could proceed without fear of those particular traps. But the lack of 
magic protection was of course still a problem. Not so much because of 
liches, because from the beginning, I had an identified scroll of 
genocide in my inventory. But there are other spellcastes and wands of 
death...

I think that three points were decisive for the success of this game. 
The first was the early altar (there was a second one on Dlvl 4, which I 
however failed to discover for a long time), the second an amulet of ESP 
found on Dlvl 1 and the third the armor shop on Dlvl 6. The amulet of 
ESP together with a blindfold (already in my inventory?) allowed careful 
proceeding on all levels that I entered via the stairs.

Continuing to sacrifice at the altar, I got a movanic deva as a pet -- 
this time I had chosen a lawful character. The second gift of my god was 
Reaper, an artifact halberd that I had to learn to use first. Not useful 
at all as a melee weapon but killing every cockatrice in one hit before 
it could even get next to my character. Since I could not hit monsters 
that I saw only by telepathy, I started casting the light spell wherever 
I went. The only pity is that boulders are supposed to block your sight, 
too, so you cannot use Reaper from inside a boulder castle protecting 
you. But I used it to clean out the front part of Grund's stronghold, 
Ludios, and the Castle. In Ludios, at the entrance door, no soldier ever 
had a chance to zap a wand at me or to throw a frag grenade, because I 
positioned myself at a knight's jump distance from the door and killed 
every entrant on the doorstep with Reaper. So I might have done it 
without reflection. Croesus was killed across the moat. Eels on the 
castle level were killed using light to make their square visible, a 
ring of warning to locate them, and Reaper. Earlier, in the lawful 
quest, I had killed eels using light, the amulet of ESP and Reaper.

The two greatest problems before Dlvl 11 were that I neither had a good 
melee weapon nor magic protection. I was even contemplating to lead my 
movanic deva to the Oracle who would surely have killed it, in order to 
get at its long sword. However, the deva protected me, too, it was a 
strong pet, so I did not consider this a good option. But dungeon level 
11 was Rats, and the rat king carried a long sword, which eventually 
became mine. Firenail moved up a level, dipped it into the fountain 
there and had Excalibur.

Dlvl 15 held the magic portal to the chaotic quest, which put me into a 
difficult situation. I entered accidentally with my hell hound pet 
Fluffy, saw an arch lich, retreated hastily, but my pet was still in 
there. That would not have been a major problem, but my movanic deva 
stupidly entered the portal afterwards and I was then without a pet. My 
+2 Excalibur would not help me survive in the chaotic quest, if I met 
Vecna, but without any strong pet at all, I was going to run into 
trouble... So I decided to bless my scroll of genocide, wiped out L, and 
went after the movanic deva of Earth. Luckily, it was next to me, when I 
passed the portal. I retreated without waiting for Fluffy, as a vampire 
was approaching me. But I had my movanic deva back.

The question then was how to get magic protection. I decided to try a 
way I had succeded once before, although with meager 70 Hp, this was 
dangerous. But with L genocided, I could attempt to get the gray dragon 
scales in the lawful quest. Unfortunately, this was the lowest of the 
three aligned quests, being at Dlvl 19. Moreover, I did not have a wand 
of cancellation, which you normally also need to perform the trick 
(found the first one in the Valley of the Dead...). But I had a potion 
of amnesia. I dealt with the rot worms in the quest using Reaper and 
casting light. I learned a new thing: Using spells of light (or 
scrolls), you can make the labyrinth on the right permanently visible 
(in the past, I had dropped one gold piece on each square to obtain the 
same effect, but sometimes a pet took away the gold). Because there are 
traps in the path, my movanic deva did not immediately follow me, and I 
had no magic whistle. I lost half of my hit points to a shadow and had 
to save myself engraving Elbereth again. Finally, I got the dragon 
scales and identified their cursed status on the altar. Dipping them 
into the potion of amnesia, I uncursed them. My hope that also the -9 
enchantment would be "forgotten", was in vain. Amnesia takes off 1 point 
of positively enchanted armor and does not change the enchantment of 
negatively enchanted one. I used my only scroll of enchant armor to get 
a -8 gray dragon scale mail. Before the procedure, my AC had been -11 
with a studded leather armor. Now it was -8 with a dragon scale mail. 
Hilarious. Nevertheless, I kept the dragon scale mail, hoping for 
further scrolls of enchant armor. Then I had an idea. I went back up to 
the armor store on Dlvl 6, where armor-works was offered. Enchanting my 
mail by one point cost only 180 zorkmids. And this price did not change 
as long as it was negatively enchanted. So I continued collecting gold 
and soon was at an AC of -16. Then the price increased to 900 zorkmids. 
Since I was level 11 by that time, this was still much cheaper for me to 
improve AC than to backtrack to the priest in mine town (where I had 
bought a few points of protection already).

I got all my armor enchanted to +1 (including, besides the water walking 
boots I wore, a spare pair of speed boots), and then, paying 3600 
zorkmids, and after and increase in charisma, 3400 zorkmids, to +2 via 
shopkeeper services. Scrolls of enchant amor were really rare, and you 
do not have to undress for shopkeeper enchantment. So I had a decent AC 
now (-27) and further enchantment was done using scrolls later.

When I found the black market (the portal was on the nymphs level), I 
had a few thousand zorkmids prepared. I wanted to buy a magic whistle. 
But all whistles in the shop were tin ones. I was somewhat desperate, 
until I realized that I could have solved my problem much earlier. I did 
have a tin whistle even from mine town (tried whether using it would 
train my pets to come to the stairs, but could not see any effect). I 
also had a potion of gain level. And now I remembered (didn't I say my 
game skills were rusty?) that you can use potions of gain level to 
upgrade objects. I dipped the tin whistle into the potion and was the 
proud owner of a magic whistle.

Using newly acquired pets to repeatedly sell gems to Sam and steal them 
again (which was now easy with a magic whistle -- I will describe the 
procedure in a different posting) I could buy quite some scrolls, 
including scrolls of charging. A magic lamp cost 75000, which was too 
much to be obtained by credit building in a short time (after all, I did 
not want to lose my pets waiting on the nymphs level), so I deposited it 
near the door in the hope, one of my two cats and dogs would pick it up. 
It was not cursed, so that happened indeed and I whistled the lamp out 
of the shop. By the way, it did not give me a wish but a tame djinn whom 
I called Maildemon and who went on to ascend with me. There was a second 
magic lamp in the Lost Tomb, which gave a wish. Actually, I was so 
well-equipped now that it was difficult to think of meaningful wishes, 
so I just asked for a ring of teleport control.

The rest of the game was not extremely eventful, in a way. I did the 
Spider Caves without retreating to heal a single time, taking three pets 
downstairs. At that point, I had two movanic devas. I was very careful 
not to displace one of them while they might be trapped (I once was 
almost killed by a ki-rin pet whom I had displaced from a spider web) 
but rather used the whistle to free them. I also destroyed some webs 
with a wand of fire, but that probably cost me a few useful scrolls. 
Girtab and Shelob fell to spells and Reaper. (I had both magic missile 
and acid stream at that time.)

I had been nervous about the Wyrm Caves but did them without major 
problem. Interestingly, it is not only the player that cannot pass 
certain narrow passages. A gray dragon and an Olog-hai could not either, 
so I could cheerfully whacked at them standing two squares away.

In the Temple of Moloch, I did most of the zombies in the entrance hall 
and left the priests to my movanic devas. This was a mistake, since most 
of their spellbooks burned. I should have cast sleeping spells and taken 
them out myself.

After some preparation, I dared to enter the chaotic quest to retrieve 
Fluffy. Surprisingly, I did not have to retame him, I could easily 
whistle him out and disappear after having killed a single vampire.

I finished the lawful quest (having done the neutral one immediately 
after getting magic protection). Since one of my flaming spheres had 
angered the priest there, I had to make sure he was killed by a pet (not 
wanting to lose telepathy). My movanic devas would not do that -- 
apparently a movanic deva of Earth will not attack a coaligned priest. 
But this time, Fluffy was with us, and he did the job. The mind flayers 
were taken out with my trusty assault rifle (my usual approach), and I 
had an amulet of flying. Nighthorn fell to the bullets as well. 
Afterwards, I soon genocided master mind flayers and later mind flayers.

On the level of the disgruntled adventurers, I switched from the amulet 
of flying to my amulet of reflection. Against wands of draining, I was 
wielding Excalibur. The only disadvantage was that I fell through a hole 
once or twice.

I skipped the Sunless Sea, because I did not need another wish.

In the castle, there was a ghoul queen that surrounded me with monsters 
twice, while I was whacking at everybody turning up in the entrance. One 
of my movanic devas had fallen to a purple worm, but I had an Archon 
from more sacrifices. He helped a lot against eels and dragons, but as I 
found out, I was now buff enough to kill dragons in melee (Excalibur was 
at +7, my AC about -35). Still I retreated using a cursed scroll of 
teleportation when the ghoul queen summoned two dragons and a minotaur 
around me. (I had teleport control from a tengu meanwhile.)

Because I am playing so slowly, I had to relearn spells several times 
from spellbooks kept in stashes.

Nothing remarkable on the chaotic quest, except possibly that I needed 
more than four rounds of five to six +5 bullets to kill Vecna. Normally, 
he should fall faster.

I returned to the quest locate level, put on the amulet of flying and 
did the quest. There is lava all around the first level, so you better 
fly or levitate. Since I once had lost a character to Maugneshaagar's 
spell casting abilities, I had checked whether the quest nemesis was a 
spell caster. Yes, the Water Mage can cast spells and what is worse, 
contrary to Maugneshaagar, he will not respect Elbereth. So I put my bag 
of holding into an oilskin sack, when I reached his level. My plan was 
to wield a potion of paralysis, teleport to him, hit him (wearing a ring 
of free action myself), switch to my assault rifle and take him out in 
two rounds. Nevertheless, I was careful enough to freeze all the squares 
around the upstairs when arriving at the level. The plan seemed to work. 
I hit him, stiffened briefly. "The Water Mage hits!" How did he recover 
so fast? Normally, paralysis works on quest nemeses! (This is the 
standard approach to Master Kaen, after all.) Well, I hit back, he 
summoned a dragon and teleported to the stairs. I killed the dragon, 
waiting for him to return. Then I teleported to the stairs myself. He 
followed suit and I fired six bullets into him, then switching to 
Excalibur, because my stethoscope told me that his remaining HP were not 
worth wasting another six bullets. He cursed me, and I felt the 
malignant aura, but killed him immediately after. Checking my inventory, 
I found that my assault rifle had gone from blessed to cursed. I wonder 
how that was possible. I thought that usually two items are hit by this 
kind of spell and a blessed item would go to uncursed only. But 
apparently it is possible to hit the same item twice. Moreover, I wonder 
whether he was aiming at the rifle. Also when dying to Maugneshaagar, it 
was my rifle that was cursed. I thought that the cursing is random?

Anyway, I was now prepared for Gehennom. In the Valley of the Dead, two 
of my dog-like pets, including Fluffy the hell hound, bit the dust. Only 
one dog was left, but not for long. The next level was Juiblex's. He did 
not even manage to swallow me. Two hits with Excalibur, and he was history.

Orcus's level came before Demogorgon's and I feared he might summon 
Demo, but casting haste self (I kept water walking boots all the time 
now) before digging a hole in the wall of his room and counting moves, I 
could appropriately time my attack to shoot at him after the hole had 
formed. A single salvo took him out. Some other monster took up the wand 
of death but could only use it once, before I killed it. The stairs to 
Frankenstein's Lab were on the level, but I skipped it.

My approach to Demogorgon, when I can get at him on his own level, is 
clear. I block the upstairs, reading an *uncursed* scroll of earth, push 
one boulder to get out of the boulder fortress, push it then back. 
Having mapped his level, I proceed to the point just before entering his 
line of sight. I ready my rifle and a cursed potion of gain level. 
Ideally, I step forward in a turn where I have two moves and use the 
first to engrave Elbereth. But usually I get the counting wrong (or 
there is some other reason, I don't know), so he is immediately next to 
me and makes me ill and possibly stunned. This time I also quaffed a 
potion of invulnerability before making my step, so at least his hits 
did not hurt me. I then engraved Elbereth and cast cure sickness (I got 
not stunned this time, but will stunning make you get Elbereth wrong?). 
Demogorgon turned to flee, and I sent him a round of bullets, so he 
teleported to the upstairs. I quaffed my potion of gain level and 
proceeded to the downstairs. After that, everything is easy, normally: 
go downstairs and shoot him. He cannot sit on the stairs, because there 
is a boulder on it. He cannot sit next to the stairs, because it is 
surrounded by boulders. So he cannot hit you, but your bullets can reach 
him. (Reaper unfortunately, cannot. But I had some 500 bullets from the 
castle, enchanted to +5. I carried only 200 and put the others in a 
stash, for weight management. Before leaving the dungeons, I refilled my 
stock.)

The other demon princes were easy, as usual. I never carry gold openly 
at that stage of the game, so there is no paying one off. However, I did 
not find Vlad's tower before finding the vibrating square. I went back 
up to Juiblex's level, searching all the levels I had not magically 
mapped. No Vlad's tower. Then I started thinking. I had magically mapped 
some levels. My scrolls of magical mapping had all been blessed, because 
of the way I acquired them. In slashem, a magic marker will not write 
many scrolls of enchant armor or weapon, as they are pretty expensive in 
terms of charges. So if my marker has only fifteen charges or so left, I 
start writing scrolls of magical mapping, which can be as cheap as four 
charges. Of course, the marker was blessed for the scrolls of enchant 
a/w, therefore all scrolls written are blessed (unless I have cursed 
blank scrolls). Now a blessed scroll of magical mapping will not only 
map the level but also detect the treasures in it. And that is the crux: 
what if a pile of gold is on the stairs? The stairs will not be visible. 
So a blessed scroll of magical mapping may be a curse rather than a 
blessing... Anyway, I started now to search all the levels I had 
magically mapped, by looking up each of those gold piles. And indeed, 
the stairs were under one of them.  Firenail killed Vlad and found his 
third amulet of life saving. No amulet of magical breathing, but he did 
not notice.

By the way, giant shoggoths were no problem whatsoever. The ring of 
warning made their detection easy, and then they were killed using 
Reaper. Interestingly, the do not move to attack, although they are 
faster than a player.

In the meantime, Firenail had advanced to level 30 by pure fighting 
(this is slashem!). So he went back to the dungeon and an altar on Dlvl 
38. He then started sacrificing again. He got an artifact (Sunsword?), 
but this was not what he was after. He continued sacrificing and 
eventually was rewarded by a minion, which, as was inevitable at this 
stage, was a Solar (wielding Demonbane). Much more valuable than an 
artifact, in fact a kind of life insurance.  The pets now included an 
Archon of Earth, a Solar of Earth, a movanic deva of Earth and 
Maildemon, the only one I could name.

Down to the wizard. Froze all his moat. Killed him with bullets. Down to 
the vibrating square. Invocation ritual. Downstairs. Upstairs. Teleport 
down a level. Bit of fighting.  (I had not heeded my power level, so 
couldn't almost cast any spells. Power surge wasn't yet available 
again.) But my pets took care of most of the priests. Some fell to 
Reaper. Killed the High Priest. Grabbed the amulet. Quaffed a cursed 
potion of levitation.

I realized that I missed one item for the ascension -- an amulet of 
magical breathing, as a life insurance on the Water level. But there was 
still a wish left in my castle wand, so no problem. Getting upwards in 
the dungeon without any major events. There is no mysterious force in 
slashem. Killed the wizard a few times, once with a wand of death, but 
also with bullets. He was somewhat distracted by the fact that my pet 
Solar kept attacking him...

Earth was uneventful. Located the portal by reading a scroll of gold 
detection while confused. The only difficulty was getting all pets ready 
for the portal. Fortunately, one or two of them could move through 
before me...  Nevertheless left the movanic deva behind at some point. 
(He was slow, due to an encounter with a shade, and zapping a wand of 
speed monster at him was ineffective.) Air was uneventful. Somehow, air 
elementals were less ferocious than I remembered them. Maybe, they could 
never swallow me before already having taken a few hits. Maybe, my AC of 
-46 helped. In any case, I did not waste more than one charge of my wand 
of death on an air elemental, usually just killing them from the inside 
with Excalibur. Their pummeling debris attack did not do much damage. 
Fire was uneventful. Before entering the portal, I switched from my 
amulet of flying to magical breathing and a ring of levitation. Water 
was boring, because it took so long for the portal to arrive. Got the 
malignant aura. My main concern was that the portal would be covered by 
an item and I would go through without my pets. But when it got close, I 
did see it and could maneuver my three pets through it.

On Astral, I had four pets again. Famine was on the right, so I went 
there first. I whistled my pets in front of myself at the entrance, and 
the Solar killed Famine. I waited some time until a few more Angels and 
priests had gone and Famine been killed a second time. Then I proceeded. 
An Archon blinded me with his radiance and got me stunned just when 
Famine was resurrected. I put on a blindfold and focused on Famine who 
was killed by a salvo of bullets. (Reaper would not work when I was 
blindfolded.) Meanwhile, my Solar occupied himself with the Archon. I 
got through, but the altar was neutral. On the way back, I had to kill 
Famine a second time and needed two salvos now. Do they get stronger on 
each revival?  The middle altar was protected by Death, so I went there 
next. Again, I put my Solar in front of me. Probably I should have 
checked his HP and maybe cast a few spells of extra healing at him. 
Since I could follow only telepathically, I do not know precisely what 
killed him. Death was close, but the Solar did not attack him, maybe 
because he was low on HP. Also there was a black dragon, but the Solar 
had a shield of reflection... Anyway, Death was coming out of the door, 
and behind him there was a second purple &! Indeed, Pestilence had come 
over to pay a visit! They were standing in a row, two squares from me. 
So I could do nothing than fire six bullets. Five were sufficient to 
kill Death, the sixth hit Pestilence. After a second salvo, the access 
was open. And there were surprisingly few monsters, so my trusty Solar 
had still done some good work. The monsters that remained were 
transported away with the help of a a wand of teleport, except for a 
giant shoggoth who waited at a knight's jump distance from a door. So in 
order to teleport him away, I would have had to come into his attack 
distance. Instead, I hit him with Reaper, which cost a few turns, 
sufficient for Death to rise from the dead. This time I needed two 
salvos to send him back there, so he may also have become stronger. Then 
I proceeded to the altar. Because a hostile priest in the temple turned 
to flee, I knew it was the right one.

I whistled in my pets and went to my reward with my Archon of Earth, 
Maildemon and the guardian angel.







